      "examAnswer": "A security attack is any action that compromises the security of information owned by an organisation, and such attacks are broadly classified as passive or active. In a passive attack the attacker merely observes the transmission without modifying it; the two forms are release of message contents, in which the attacker reads the information, and traffic analysis, in which the attacker studies the pattern of communication even when the contents are encrypted. Passive attacks are very difficult to detect because no data is altered, but they are comparatively easy to prevent by the use of encryption. In an active attack the attacker modifies the data stream or creates a false stream; the principal forms are masquerade, replay, modification of messages and denial of service. Active attacks are easier to detect but difficult to prevent completely, and the emphasis is therefore on detection and recovery. Security threats may also be categorised as interruption, interception, modification and fabrication, which attack availability, confidentiality, integrity and authenticity respectively.",
