      "examAnswer": "Cryptography is the science of transforming information into an unintelligible form so that it may be transmitted securely over an insecure channel. The original message is called plaintext and the transformed message is called ciphertext; the process of converting plaintext into ciphertext is encryption and the reverse process is decryption, both being controlled by a value called the key, while the algorithm itself is called the cipher. Cryptography is classified as symmetric or asymmetric. In symmetric key cryptography a single secret key, shared by the sender and receiver, is used for both encryption and decryption; it is computationally very fast but suffers from the problem of securely distributing the key, and for n users it requires n(n−1)/2 keys. Examples are DES, Triple DES and AES. In asymmetric or public key cryptography each user possesses a pair of mathematically related keys, a public key which may be freely distributed and a private key which is kept secret; data encrypted with one key can be decrypted only with the other. It solves the key distribution problem and enables digital signatures, but is considerably slower, and RSA is the best known example. Practical systems therefore use a hybrid scheme in which asymmetric cryptography exchanges a symmetric session key that then encrypts the actual data.",
