      "examAnswer": "A block cipher is a symmetric cipher that operates on a fixed-length group of bits called a block, whereas a stream cipher operates on one bit or one byte at a time. The Data Encryption Standard is a symmetric block cipher adopted as a standard in 1977 which encrypts a plaintext block of 64 bits and produces a ciphertext block of 64 bits. Its key is stored in 64 bits, of which every eighth bit is a parity bit, giving an effective key length of 56 bits. DES uses a Feistel structure consisting of an initial permutation, sixteen identical rounds and a final permutation. In each round the block is divided into two halves, the right half is expanded, combined with a 48-bit round subkey derived from the main key, passed through eight substitution boxes which provide confusion, permuted to provide diffusion, and then combined with the left half before the halves are exchanged. DES is no longer considered secure because a 56-bit key can be recovered by exhaustive search with modern hardware. Double DES was found to be vulnerable to the meet-in-the-middle attack, and Triple DES was therefore adopted, in which DES is applied three times in the sequence encrypt with the first key, decrypt with the second and encrypt with the third, giving an effective key length of 112 or 168 bits. The encrypt–decrypt–encrypt order allows Triple DES to interoperate with single DES when all three keys are identical. Triple DES is about three times slower than DES and has now largely been replaced by the Advanced Encryption Standard.",
