      "teacher": "Encryption hides <em>what</em> you wrote. But how does the bank know the instruction really came from you, and that you cannot walk in tomorrow and say 'I never sent that'? That needs a different tool — one that works like a signature on paper, but is impossible to forge.",
      "explain": "A <strong>digital signature</strong> is a cryptographic value attached to a message that proves who sent it and that it has not been altered. The sender computes a <strong>hash</strong> of the message and encrypts that hash with their <strong>private key</strong>. Anyone can verify it using the sender's <strong>public key</strong>. It guarantees <strong>authentication, integrity and non-repudiation</strong> — but not confidentiality.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "A digital signature uses asymmetric cryptography <strong>in reverse</strong>: sign with the <em>private</em> key, verify with the <em>public</em> key.",
