      "mistake": "The most common error is reversing the keys. To <em>encrypt</em> a secret for someone you use <strong>their public key</strong>. To <em>sign</em> something you use <strong>your own private key</strong>. And never write that a digital signature keeps the message secret — it does not; it only proves who sent it and that it is unchanged.",
      "examAnswer": "A digital signature is a cryptographic mechanism that authenticates the originator of a message and guarantees that the message has not been altered. To sign a message, the sender first applies a one-way hash function to it, producing a fixed-length message digest, and then encrypts this digest with the sender's own private key; the encrypted digest forms the digital signature and is transmitted with the message. To verify the signature, the receiver decrypts it using the sender's public key to obtain the original digest, independently computes the hash of the received message, and compares the two values. If they are identical the message is accepted as genuine. A digital signature therefore provides authentication, since only the sender possesses the private key; integrity, since any modification alters the hash; and non-repudiation, since the sender cannot subsequently deny having signed the message. It does not, however, provide confidentiality, for which the message must additionally be encrypted with the receiver's public key. A digital certificate issued by a Certificate Authority binds a public key to the identity of its owner and is used to establish trust in the public key.",
