      "title": "Firewalls: Design and Types",
      "teacher": "Every college has a gate with a guard. The guard does not know what each student intends to do — he just checks who may come in and who may not, and turns the rest away. A firewall is that guard, standing between your trusted network and the wild internet.",
      "explain": "A <strong>firewall</strong> is a hardware or software system placed between a trusted internal network and an untrusted external network, which examines all traffic passing through and <strong>allows or blocks</strong> it according to a set of security rules. The main types are <strong>packet filtering, stateful inspection</strong> and <strong>proxy (application gateway)</strong> firewalls.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "A firewall enforces a <strong>security policy</strong>: all traffic in or out must pass through it and only authorised traffic is allowed.",
