      "examAnswer": "A firewall is a hardware or software system placed at the boundary between a trusted internal network and an untrusted external network, which examines all traffic passing between them and permits or blocks it in accordance with a defined security policy. All traffic entering or leaving the network must pass through the firewall, and only traffic authorised by the policy is allowed. A packet filtering firewall operates at the network and transport layers and takes decisions on the basis of the source and destination IP addresses, port numbers and protocol contained in each packet header; it is stateless, very fast and inexpensive, but cannot detect spoofed packets. A stateful inspection firewall additionally maintains a table of active connections and permits an incoming packet only if it belongs to a legitimately established connection, offering considerably greater security. A proxy or application gateway firewall operates at the application layer and terminates the connection, establishing a separate connection on behalf of the client, which enables it to examine the actual content of the traffic and filter it by URL or file type; it is the most secure but also the slowest type. Common firewall architectures include the screening router, the bastion host, the dual-homed host and the screened subnet, in which a demilitarised zone containing public servers is placed between two firewalls.",
